course: dictionary
lesson: 4035
title: GDP: the size of an economy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: GDP is an abbreviation. The letters stand for gross domestic product, and it is a single number that shows how much a country makes in one year. It counts goods, which are things like cars and bread, and services, which are help people pay for, like haircuts and teaching.
explain: Think of a country as one big workshop. GDP adds up the value of everything it makes in a year. A bigger GDP means a bigger economy.
explain: «A recession is when a country's economy shrinks for a while.» When GDP falls, the economy shrinks. Shrinking by 1% turns a £100 economy into £99, which is a small drop, but it is still a drop.
explain: Dividing GDP by the number of people gives GDP per person, a rough idea of how well off people are on average. In the news, «GDP grew by 2%» means the economy made 2% more than the year before.
fact: GDP stands for gross domestic product.
fact: GDP is the total value of all the goods and services a country makes in one year.
fact: GDP is often used to compare how big countries' economies are.
fact: GDP per person is GDP divided by the number of people in a country.
fact: «Recession» means a time when a country's economy shrinks for a while.
exercise: What does GDP stand for? | *Gross domestic product | General sales payment | Great deal of prices | Gross daily profit
exercise: «GDP grew by 2%» means what? | The country's population grew by 2% | Prices fell by 2% | The economy made 2% less than the year before | *The economy made 2% more than the year before
exercise: Using the example, a £100 economy shrinks by 1%. What is it worth now? | *£99 | £90 | £100 | £101
test: GDP is the total value of what? | Roads and railways only | People living in a country | Money kept in banks | *Goods and services a country makes in one year
test: How do you find GDP per person? | *Divide GDP by the number of people | Add GDP and the population | Subtract the prices | Multiply GDP by 2
exam: Why might GDP be useful when comparing two countries? | *It gives a rough idea of how big each economy is | It shows how many languages are spoken | The colour of their flags shows which is bigger | It shows the number of schools only
